Creating Custom Application Pod

Suppose you pushed your custom image to Docker Hub:

swaphub92/netflix-app

Create Pod:

->kubectl run myapp --image=swaphub92/netflix-app

Check:

->kubectl get pods

Describe:

->kubectl describe pod myapp
Why Can't We Access the Application?

The question now is 

"My Pod is running. Why can't I open it in browser?"

Because Pods are accessible only inside the Kubernetes network.

Example:

Internet
   ❌
   │
Pod IP
10.42.0.5

Pod IP is internal.

How Do We Access the Application?

We create a Service.

Think of a Service as a door to reach the Pod.

Laptop Browser
      │
      ▼
   Service
      │
      ▼
     Pod
      │
      ▼
 Application

Without a Service, users outside Kubernetes cannot reach the Pod.

Expose the Pod

Create a Service using:

kubectl expose pod myapp \
--type=NodePort \
--port=8080 \
--target-port=8080

This command creates a Service for the Pod.

Understanding the Command
target-port-->The port on which the application is running inside the container.

For example:

Tomcat → 8080

So:
--target-port=8080

means send traffic to port 8080 inside the container.

port

The Service port.

Service → 8080
        ↓
Container → 8080
--port=8080
type=NodePort

This is the most important part.

NodePort opens a port on the Kubernetes Node (VM).

Laptop Browser
       │
       ▼
Node IP : 192.168.1.10
Port    : 32193
       │
       ▼
Service
       │
       ▼
Pod

Now users can access the application from outside Kubernetes.

Check Service
->kubectl get svc

Example output:

NAME    TYPE       PORT(S)
myapp   NodePort   8080:32193

Meaning:

Service Port = 8080
NodePort     = 32193

Kubernetes automatically selected port 32193.

Access Application in Browser

First find VM IP:

hostname -i

Example:

192.168.1.10

Then open:

http://192.168.1.10:32193

Flow:

Browser
   │
   ▼
VM IP:32193
   │
   ▼
Service
   │
   ▼
Pod
   │
   ▼
Tomcat Application

Now the application running inside the Pod becomes accessible in your laptop browser.
